.\" The protocol of aqr --ipc, in mdoc(7). The implementation is crates/aqr/src/ipc.rs (the
.\" requests and the run), session.rs (inventory) and serve.rs (the archives browsed), and the
.\" frame crates/aqr-ipc/src/wire.rs, which the frontends' side (crates/aqr-ipc) shares. Keep
.\" them together.
.Dd November 28, 2026
.Dt AQRIPC 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm aqripc
.Nd the protocol by which a program drives aqr
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm aqr
.Op Ar options
.Fl -ipc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Ql aqr --ipc
is driven by another program (a graphical frontend, a mount) through its standard input and
output, both ways at once.
It takes no inputs: the work comes with its requests.
The options of its command line (such as
.Fl T
and
.Fl Z )
are those of everything it serves.
Its standard error is a log for a person, not part of the protocol.
.Pp
This is version 2 of the protocol.
Until Aqueous 1.0, a version may change in ways that are not compatible, and its number is
raised; after, it only grows (new requests, new fields), its number raised where anything is
taken away or changes its meaning.
.Ss Messages
A message is a line of JSON, an object, and where it has
.Ql \(dqblob\(dq: Ns Ar N
at its top,
.Ar N
bytes right after the line's newline, as they are (no encoding).
No other field is so read, at any depth: a field of the same name inside an object is only data.
Both sides send messages so.
A line that is not an object of JSON is ignored.
.Pp
A request is
.Ql {\(dqq\(dq: Ns Ar N , Ns \(dqop\(dq: Ns Ar OP , Ns ...} ,
numbered by the side that sends it (each side numbers its own: the numbers of the two sides are
unrelated).
Its replies are
.Ql {\(dqr\(dq: Ns Ar N , Ns ...} ,
each but the last with
.Ql \(dqmore\(dq:true ;
the last has no
.Ql more .
Replies of different requests come in any order.
A request that fails has as its last reply
.Ql {\(dqr\(dq: Ns Ar N , Ns \(dqerror\(dq:{\(dqkind\(dq: Ns Ar K , Ns \(dqmessage\(dq: Ns Ar M Ns }} ,
.Ar K
a word for programs (see
.Sx ERRORS ) ,
.Ar M
a sentence in English, which a frontend that does not know
.Ar K
shows as it is.
.Pp
A path is a string, or where it is not text (a name of Windows of a lone surrogate, a name of
Unix not of UTF-8)
.Ql {\(dqraw\(dq: Ns Ar HEX Ns } :
on Windows its units of UTF-16, little-endian, else its bytes.
Names inside archives are strings, of
.Ql /
between them, as
.Ql aqr -d
writes them.
.Ss Hello
The frontend's first message is its hello, which aqr answers before anything else:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
> {"hello":2,"answers":["overwrite","passphrase"],"chunk":4194304}
< {"hello":2,"ops":["run","inventory","open",...],"chunk":4194304}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql answers
are the kinds of question the frontend answers (see
.Sx QUESTIONS ) ;
aqr asks no others.
.Ql chunk
is the most bytes of a blob the frontend wants, between 1 MiB (the default) and 4 MiB; aqr
answers what it takes.
.Ql ops
are the requests it serves.
A frontend of an older version than aqr knows is refused: aqr ends, telling so on its standard
error.
.Pp
Standard input closed cancels the run where one is at work (as
.Ic cancel
does), else ends aqr.
.Sh REQUESTS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic run Pq Ar args
Does what
.Nm aqr
would with the arguments
.Ar args
(an array of strings, or paths as above), once a process.
Its replies tell how far it is
.Pq see Sx EVENTS ;
its last is
.Ql {\(dqexit\(dq: Ns Ar CODE } ,
as the process ends with that code.
A second run is refused
.Pq Ql busy ;
the requests of the other kinds go on being served while one is at work.
.It Ic inventory Pq Ar args
Gathers the inputs of a compression of
.Ar args
ahead of it, as a dialog of options asks while its user chooses them (only
.Fl x ,
.Fl L ,
.Fl p ,
.Fl -rm
and the inputs bear on it).
Its replies, now and then, have
.Ql files
(found so far),
.Ql bytes
(of those read so far),
.Ql walked
(whether the directories are all walked) and
.Ql done ,
true of the last.
A new inventory replaces the one at work, whose last reply is then the error
.Ql cancelled ;
of the same inputs, it is the same work, replied to the new request.
A run of the same inputs takes what was gathered, or waits for it where it is at work.
.It Ic cancel Pq Ar of , Op Ar finish
Cancels request
.Ar of :
a run, its output being written removed (with
.Ar finish
true, an archive being written is ended as it is, of the files written; an extraction is always
ended so, the file being written removed); an inventory; a read, before its next reply.
Its reply is empty.
.It Ic open Pq Ar path , Op Ar passphrase , Op Ar at
Opens the archive at
.Ar path ,
by its metadata alone: its reply has
.Ql archive ,
a handle the other requests name,
.Ql identity
(the file opened, by volume, number, size and time),
.Ql single
(an archive of a single file, whose one entry is the name it is extracted as) and, of an
archive of another format,
.Ql fields ,
what its entries may have.
The same file opened again while it is open is the same handle (closed as many times); a file
changed at the path is opened anew,
.Ql changed
true, the handle before staying of the file it was.
An encrypted archive is opened of
.Ar passphrase
(else the error
.Ql encrypted ,
or
.Ql wrong-passphrase ) .
.Ar at ,
the
.Ql metadata
of a journal point, opens the archive as it was then, as
.Fl -at
does.
Archives of other formats that
.Nm aqr
reads (ZIP, 7z, RAR, tar, ...) and single files compressed are opened alike.
.It Ic list Pq Ar archive , Ar dir , Op Ar verbose
The entries of directory
.Ar dir
(names joined by
.Ql / ,
empty for the root), in replies of at most 4096
.Ql entries .
An entry has its
.Ql name ,
its
.Ql kind
.Pq Cm dir , Cm file , Cm link , Cm other
and what is known of it: a file's
.Ql size ,
.Ql mtime
(seconds and nanoseconds since 1970;
.Ql local
true of a local time of no known zone;
.Ql crtime ,
.Ql atime ,
.Ql ctime
alike),
.Ql attrs ,
.Ql owner ,
.Ql mode ,
.Ql win ,
.Ql mac ,
.Ql flags ,
.Ql comment ,
a link's
.Ql target .
With
.Ar verbose
true, what
.Ql aqr -t -v
tells of a file too
.Pq Cm data , Cm overhead , Cm share , Cm ratio , Cm segment , Cm formula , Cm nodes .
.It Ic stat Pq Ar archive , Ar path , Op Ar verbose
The entry at
.Ar path ,
as
.Ql entry
(the root's of no name).
.It Ic read Pq Ar archive , Ar path , Ar offset , Ar len
The bytes of the file at
.Ar path
from
.Ar offset ,
at most
.Ar len
(fewer only at the end of the file, none past it), as the blobs of its replies, each at most
the hello's
.Ql chunk .
The first read of a file decodes all of what it is decoded with, which is kept for the reads
after (in memory, or spilled to files past a bound,
.Fl Z Cm serve:cache ) .
.It Ic close Pq Ar archive
Gives the handle back; once all of a file's are, the archive is let go of.
.It Ic journal Pq Ar archive
Its journal points (its named versions), as
.Ql points :
each its
.Ql metadata
(what
.Ic open
takes as
.Ar at ) ,
.Ql time
(milliseconds since 1970),
.Ql shown
and
.Ql label .
.It Ic progress Pq Ar archive
How far the decoding of its files is:
.Ql decompressed
and
.Ql evaluated ,
.Ql [ Ns Ar done , Ar total ]
bytes, and
.Ql evaluating .
.It Ic trim
The files kept dropped:
.Ql trimmed ,
their bytes.
.El
.Sh EVENTS
The replies of a run, each with
.Ql event :
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm begin
An archive to make
.Pq Ar mode Cm compress
or decode
.Pq Cm decompress , Cm test ,
of
.Ql inputs
into
.Ql output
(null testing, and with
.Fl -auto-dir
where it is not decided yet).
.It Cm output
With
.Fl -auto-dir ,
what the archive is extracted as, once decided.
.It Cm progress
How far it is, a few times a second: compressing,
.Ql probe ,
.Ql analysis
and
.Ql compression ,
each
.Ql [ Ns Ar done , Ar total ]
(files of the probe, bytes otherwise), and while the files are listed
.Ql listed
.Pq Ql [ Ns Ar files , Ar bytes ] ,
.Ql read
and
.Ql dir ;
decoding,
.Ql decompressed
and
.Ql evaluated
(bytes); converting an Aqueous archive,
.Ql written .
With
.Ql file ,
where one is worked on long:
.Ql {\(dqpath\(dq,\(dqseconds\(dq}
(and its
.Ql len
and
.Ql done
where told).
.It Cm end
An archive done:
.Ql output ,
.Ql files ,
.Ql read ,
.Ql written
and
.Ql seconds .
.It Cm skipped
An archive not made, as the frontend answered
.Pq Ql output .
.It Cm notice
Something to tell of an archive made that does not fail it:
.Ql kind ,
.Ql output ,
.Ql message .
Of kind
.Ql unsigned :
a self-extracting program is not signed.
.It Cm error
The failure that the run ends with
.Pq Ql message .
.It Cm stopped
Stopped as the frontend asked; of an archive ended as it was,
.Ql kept :
.Ql {\(dqfiles\(dq,\(dqbytes\(dq}
it holds, or null.
.El
.Sh QUESTIONS
Where the options leave something open, aqr asks the frontend by a request of its own, and
the work that asks waits for its reply:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
< {"q":1,"op":"ask","job":7,"kind":"overwrite","path":"x.aqr"}
> {"r":1,"value":true}
.Ed
.Pp
.Ql job
is the run's request.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm overwrite Pq Ar path
Whether the output at
.Ar path ,
there already, is written over (true) or the archive skipped.
.It Cm passphrase Pq Ar path , Ar mode , Ar again , Ar confirm
The passphrase (a string, empty or not), or null to give up: compressing with
.Fl P
.Pq Ar mode Cm compress , Ar path No null , to be typed twice as Ar confirm No tells ;
decoding, as
.Fl P
begins or as archive
.Ar path
needs one,
.Ar again
where the one before was wrong; converting, the input's, then the new one's
.Pq Ar mode Ql convert-new .
.El
.Pp
Each kind but
.Cm passphrase
has an option that answers it before it is asked
.Pf ( Cm overwrite : Fl f ) ,
and where the frontend does not take a kind, aqr does as it does with no one to ask.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm bad-request
A request of missing or wrong fields, or of arguments
.Nm aqr
does not take.
.It Cm not-known
A request of no kind served.
.It Cm busy
A second run, or no thread for the request.
.It Cm cancelled
Cancelled, or replaced.
.It Cm no-archive
No archive open of the handle.
.It Cm not-found
No such file, or no such entry in the archive.
.It Cm not-a-directory , Cm not-a-file
An entry of another kind than the request's.
.It Cm encrypted , Cm wrong-passphrase
An archive encrypted, opened of no passphrase or of a wrong one.
.It Cm failed
Anything else.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A compression dialog: the inputs counted while the options are chosen, then the run.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
> {"hello":2,"answers":["overwrite"]}
< {"hello":2,"ops":[...],"chunk":1048576}
> {"q":1,"op":"inventory","args":["-c","--","D:\e\ea"]}
< {"r":1,"more":true,"files":120,"bytes":3400000,"walked":false,"done":false}
< {"r":1,"files":1400,"bytes":61000000,"walked":true,"done":true}
> {"q":2,"op":"run","args":["-c","-l","7","-o","D:\e\ea.aqr","--","D:\e\ea"]}
< {"r":2,"more":true,"event":"begin","mode":"compress",...}
< {"r":2,"more":true,"event":"progress",...}
< {"r":2,"more":true,"event":"end",...}
< {"r":2,"exit":0}
.Ed
.Pp
A file of an archive read, in replies of the chunk:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
> {"q":1,"op":"open","path":"x.aqr"}
< {"r":1,"archive":1,"identity":"...","single":false}
> {"q":2,"op":"read","archive":1,"path":"a/b.bin","offset":0,"len":3000000}
< {"r":2,"more":true,"blob":1048576}   followed by its bytes
< {"r":2,"more":true,"blob":1048576}   ...
< {"r":2,"blob":902848}                ...
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr aqr 1
